      "title": "Chemistry Adjunct Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2024-11-06\n\nLocation: Orangeburg\n\nAdjunct Lecturer in Chemistry   Responsibilities: Teach introductory, undergraduate laboratory-based courses including General Chemistry, Introductory Biochemistry, and/or Organic Chemistry.  Requirements: Terminal or advanced degree (master's degree minimum) with professional experience required; College teaching and clinical experience preferred. Fluency in Spanish is preferred, but not required. Dominican University New York, located in Orangeburg, New York, is 17 miles north of New York City, with approximately 1,400 students and 400 faculty and staff.   There is a wide range of academic programs for students to choose from with more than 30 undergraduate and graduate programs in business, education, liberal arts, nursing, occupational therapy, organizational leadership and communication, and physical therapy. The independent, four-year, co-ed university was founded in 1952 by the Dominican Sisters of Blauvelt in the Catholic tradition. It is chartered by the Board of Regents of the University of the State of New York and fully accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education. Dominican University New York has been named a College of Distinction, is designated a Hispanic Serving Institution, and is recognized as Military Friendly. Dominican University New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action employer committed to excellence through equity and inclusion, and, in this spirit, particularly welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented groups. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, status as a protected veteran, status as a qualified individual with a disability, or other protected category in accordance with applicable law. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. AA/EOE  $2,760/course (lecture only); $4,600 (lecture and lab)",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=5343bd75-9ae4-4e31-8242-ccbb66b5305b&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9200598469885_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Athletic Director for Compliance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nLocation: Orangeburg\n\nAssistant Athletic Director for Compliance  The Assistant Athletic Director for Compliance serves as the institution's primary administrator responsible for ensuring compliance with NCAA Division II, conference, and institutional regulations. The position develops, implements, and monitors comprehensive compliance systems related to recruiting, eligibility, financial aid, amateurism, playing and practice seasons, Name, Image and Likeness (NIL), and student-athlete welfare. The Assistant Athletic Director for Compliance works collaboratively with athletics staff, Faculty, Admissions, Student Financial Services, Registrar, Academic Support Center, and university leadership to maintain a culture of compliance and integrity. Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree is required. Strong organization and leadership skills; thorough knowledge of NCAA rules and regulations; demonstrated commitment to gender equity and diversity .  2 – 3 years' experience in athletic administration and /or coaching at the college level. Responsibilities: Serve as the College’s NCAA compliance coordinator. Administer the institution's NCAA Division II compliance program and monitor athletics activities to ensure compliance with NCAA regulations. Interpret NCAA, conference, and institutional rules and provide guidance to coaches, staff, and student-athletes Develop and maintain compliance policies, procedures, and educational materials. Investigate and document potential violations and coordinate reporting when required. Coordinate initial and continuing eligibility certification for all student-athletes. Maintain squad lists and required NCAA eligibility documentation. Manage the transfer eligibility review processes. Review countable aid, institutional grants, and outside awards affecting NCAA eligibility. Monitor recruiting activities, official visits, unofficial visits, and recruiting communications. Ensure recruiting practices comply with NCAA and CACC regulations. Develop and conduct ongoing compliance education programs for coaches, student-athletes, athletics staff. Maintain compliance databases and software systems. Prepare and submit NCAA and CACC reports. Meet with coaches regularly to ensure NCAA rule compliance as well as institutional guidelines are adhered to. Serve as the penalty box supervisor for all home men's lacrosse games. Serve as a designated Game Manager Supervisor. Assist the Associate Athletic Director with budget oversight. Performs other duties as assigned. Dominican University New York is a private not-for-profit institution. The aim of Dominican University New York is to promote educational excellence, leadership, and service in an environment characterized by respect for the individual and concern for the community and its needs. Founded by the Dominican Sisters of Blauvelt, the University is an independent institution of higher learning, Catholic in origin and heritage. In the Dominican tradition, it fosters the active, shared pursuit of truth and embodies an ideal of education rooted in the values of reflective understanding and compassionate involvement. Committed to building its programs upon a strong foundation in the liberal arts and sciences, the University maintains a student-centered climate and serves a diverse community of students in undergraduate and graduate programs.  Dominican University New York is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action employer committed to excellence through equity and inclusion, and, in this spirit, particularly welcomes applications from members of historically underrepresented groups. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, age, status as a protected veteran, status as a qualified individual with a disability, or other protected category in accordance with applicable law. AA/EOE Salary Range: $67,000 - $71,000",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
